rsx draw engine: a converted-vertex cache; a repeated draw is not fetched, converted and staged again.

The draw engine converts every draw's vertices to compact float4 vertices on the RSX thread: it reads the indices,
collapses repeated references, decodes each attribute, rebuilds the topology into an index list and copies all of it
into the backend's staging arena. In Resistance 3's Haven firefight that thread is the one the game waits on (the main
PPU thread polls a GCM label in usleep(50), func_0073439C, 60% of its time) and it is busy 100% of the time; a third
of it went to vertex work (rsx_vertex_remap_build, rsx_vertex_fetch_many, eng_stage_copy's memcpy). A census of the
fight (debug counters in dc_fetch) found ~7800 draws a frame, 2.3M vertex references, 42 MB of converted vertices,
and 95-97% of the draws identical, byte for byte in indices, layout, attribute state and vertex data, to a draw of
the same or the previous frame.

The cache (rsx_vertex_compact.c/.h, rsx_vc_*) keeps a draw's converted vertices and rebuilt indices in memory the GPU
reads, keyed by a 128-bit hash (wyhash's multiply step in four lanes; a difference reaches the key through one 64-bit
lane, so two inputs collide with a probability of about 2^-64) of everything they are made from:
- ikey: primitive, layout, batches, index array state (base index, restart) and the index bytes; it also remembers
  the element range the indices span, so a repeated draw does not read its indices one by one;
- vkey: ikey plus every enabled attribute's state (format, offsets, default values, divider) and the bytes of the
  element range, each byte once even when attributes are interleaved.
A changed byte is a different key, so nothing is invalidated: unused entries age out. Memory comes from the backend
as 32 MB chunks (RSX_DRAW_ENGINE_VCACHE_MB, default 256), split by a buddy allocator into 256-byte-aligned blocks; a
block is reused only after the submission that last read it has completed on the GPU (commit serials). Draws the
key cannot describe take the old path: inline arrays, immediate vertices, per-instance or divider attributes, an
index or vertex range that does not resolve to host memory, a vertex range more than 4x what the draw reads.

The backend interface gains four optional entries (vcache_chunk_create/_release, draw_cached, commit_serial); the
Metal backend implements them (shared MTLBuffers, a record field naming the chunk, a completion handler per engine
command buffer). A backend without them, and PS3RECOMP_ENG_VCACHE=0, keep the old path; PS3RECOMP_ENG_VCACHE_STATS=1
prints the hit rate every 300 frames.

What the key leaves out matters as much as what it has. Two versions that keyed more missed 15-20% of the fight's
draws (counted in the stats line as "changed vertices": indices known, vertex key new): the vertex buffers' addresses
(dropped: the bytes and each attribute's offset among them are keyed instead) and above all the attributes' current
values. The game sets current values between draws whose arrays override them; a current value only reaches a vertex
for an attribute with no array or of a type the decoder does not know, and only then is it keyed.

Measured in the Haven firefight (scripted boot to the fight, 280 s runs, `sample` of 10 s in the fight, other
processes loading the machine alike, load average 22-25):
- hits 99.5% of the cacheable draws (23.48M of 23.60M lookups; 0.35% of draws bypassed), 75-250 MB in use, no
  insert failures;
- the RSX thread (the present/walker thread) idles 21% of the samples, against 0% before; rsx_vertex_remap_build,
  rsx_vertex_fetch_many and their eng_stage_copy left the profile; rsx_vc_hash costs 3.3% of the thread;
- 5 s windows of the fight: mean 20.3 fps against 17.8, 5 windows with a frame over 200 ms against 11 (fight
  timing differs between runs; the profile shares are the firmer figure);
- frames compared by eye at 200/230/260 s: geometry, HUD, hurt overlay as before.

Tests: libs/video/tests/test_vertex_cache.c (hash sensitivity, find/insert, a block in use by an incomplete
submission is not reused, buddy blocks coalesce, a budget under one chunk shrinks the chunks, a random stress with
GPU latency: hot keys stay cached, no overlapping live blocks, no early reuse), libs/video/tests/
test_engine_vertex_cache.c (through the stub backend: a repeat is a hit on the same block; each use is tagged with
the submission it goes into; a changed vertex byte, index or vertex format converts again and draws the new data; the
old bytes find the old copy; a per-instance attribute, the switch and a backend without hooks keep the staged path)
and libs/video/tests/test_metal_vertex_cache.m (chunks are shared buffers, a cached draw records its chunk, every
engine command buffer takes the announced serial and reports it completed). Added to CMake (vertex_cache) and to the
macOS/Linux workflow steps.

Upstream: PR #718 (rebased onto master without this domain's earlier patches; its Metal test there leaves the record cap to #719).
